const body = `<h1>Searching &amp; Sorting</h1>

<p>
    CED topics 4.14 and 4.15. You wrote the loop versions in 7.3; this page is
    the complete reference, plus the two ideas that make the topic click: the
    precondition contract of binary search, and the trade between the two sorts.
</p>

<h2>Linear Search</h2>

<pre><code>// returns index of first match, or -1
public static int linearSearch(ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt; list, int target) {
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; list.size(); i++) {
        if (list.get(i) == target) return i;
    }
    return -1;
}</code></pre>

<ul>
    <li>Works on ANY data, sorted or not. No preconditions.</li>
    <li>Best case 1 step (target first). Worst case n (target last or missing).
    On average, about n/2. All linear.</li>
    <li>Finds the FIRST occurrence. To find all occurrences, drop the early
    return and collect.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Binary Search</h2>

<pre><code>// PRECONDITION: list is sorted ascending. Returns an index of target, or -1.
public static int binarySearch(ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt; list, int target) {
    int lo = 0, hi = list.size() - 1;
    while (lo &lt;= hi) {
        int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
        if (list.get(mid) == target)     return mid;
        else if (list.get(mid) &lt; target) lo = mid + 1;
        else                             hi = mid - 1;
    }
    return -1;
}</code></pre>

<ul>
    <li>Each iteration throws away half the remaining range. 1000 items: 10
    steps max. A million: 20. A billion: 30.</li>
    <li>The loop invariant: the target, if present, is always between lo and hi.
    Every line of the method exists to keep that sentence true.</li>
    <li>When lo crosses hi, the range is empty and the answer is "not here."</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout trap">
    <strong>The precondition is part of the algorithm.</strong> Binary search on
    unsorted data does not crash. It CONFIDENTLY RETURNS GARBAGE. The exam
    loves this: "the list is not sorted, what does this binary search return?"
    The honest answer is "an index that may be wrong, and -1 is possible even
    when the target exists." A sorted-list precondition is a contract, and
    violating it silently is the most exam-testable fact in this topic.
</div>

<h2>Trace Table: Watch It Halve</h2>

<p>List <code>[3, 7, 11, 15, 19, 25, 31, 39]</code>, target 25:</p>

<table>
    <tr><th>Step</th><th>lo</th><th>hi</th><th>mid</th><th>list[mid]</th><th>Action</th></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>7</td><td>3</td><td>15</td><td>15 &lt; 25, go right: lo = 4</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>7</td><td>5</td><td>25</td><td>found! return 5</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Two steps. Linear search needed six. Now target 8 (absent):</p>

<table>
    <tr><th>Step</th><th>lo</th><th>hi</th><th>mid</th><th>list[mid]</th><th>Action</th></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>7</td><td>3</td><td>15</td><td>15 &gt; 8, go left: hi = 2</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>0</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>7</td><td>7 &lt; 8, go right: lo = 2</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>11</td><td>11 &gt; 8, go left: hi = 1</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td></td><td></td><td>lo &gt; hi: empty range, return -1</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Selection Sort</h2>

<p>Find the smallest in the unsorted region, swap it to the front, shrink the
region. The full treatment with traces, code, and the insertion sort comparison
lives in <a href="sorting.html">Sorting</a>. Summary for this page:</p>

<table>
    <tr><th></th><th>Selection sort</th><th>Insertion sort</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Comparisons</td><td>always ~n²/2</td><td>n-1 best (sorted input), ~n²/2 worst</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Moves</td><td>at most n-1 swaps</td><td>up to ~n²/2 shifts</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Shines when</td><td>swaps are expensive (big objects)</td><td>data is nearly sorted</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Both</td><td colspan="2">quadratic overall. Correct, simple, slow at scale.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Binary Search + Sorting = One System</h2>

<p>The trade: sorting once costs ~n²/2 (selection) or better, and buys log n
searches forever after. If you search once, linear wins (sort costs more than
it saves). If you search many times against a growing dataset, sort (or keep
sorted on insert) and binary search wins massively. Merge sort (10.3) is the
better sort, and 10.6 gives you its recursive search partner. This pricing
logic is exactly what 4.7a's run-time analysis is for.</p>

<h2>Tinker Challenge: The Insertion Search</h2>

<div class="tinker">
    <h3>Keep It Sorted As You Build</h3>
    <p>Instead of loading monsters and sorting after: insert each monster into
    its correct sorted position as you read the file (your 6.3b insert
    algorithm, upgraded to ArrayList). Benefits: the list is always
    binary-searchable, mid-load. Costs: every insert shifts elements, so
    building is quadratic in the worst case. Build the same bestiary both ways
    and count total element-moves with a counter. Which wins for 8 monsters?
    Would the answer change for 8000? Defend with shapes, not vibes.</p>
</div>

<h2>Bug Hunt</h2>

<div class="bug-hunt">
    <h3>Three Broken Searches</h3>
    <pre><code>// A: the off-by-one forever loop
while (lo &lt; hi) {                    // should be &lt;=
    ...
    else lo = mid;                    // should be mid + 1
}

// B: the skipper
else if (list.get(mid) &lt; target) lo = mid + 2;   // +2 jumps over a candidate

// C: the amnesiac
return -1;    // inside the loop, after the first comparison</code></pre>
    <p>For each: pick the symptom (infinite loop / wrong index / always -1 /
    missed elements), then run it to confirm. The infinite one will print your
    own debug lines forever; kill it and note how Java reports
    StackOverflowError versus just hanging. Some bugs are loud. The dangerous
    ones are B and C: they finish, and they lie.</p>
</div>

<h2>Socratic Checkpoint</h2>

<div class="socratic">
    <h3>Why does the exam prefer tracing binary search to writing it?</h3>
    <p>Because the trace proves the invariant, and the invariant is the
    understanding. "If it exists, it is in [lo, hi]" is a sentence you can
    check by hand on every row of a trace table. Writing the code from memory
    is 10.6's job; reading a stranger's binary search and predicting its
    behavior, including its failure modes, is the exam-level skill.</p>
    <h3>When is -1 a lie?</h3>
    <p>When the list was not sorted. -1 means "not in the list" only under the
    precondition. The exam's sneakiest questions hand you an unsorted list and
    a correct-looking binary search, and ask what it returns for a present
    element. Sometimes it accidentally finds it. Sometimes -1. You must TRACE,
    not assume. That is the whole lesson of preconditions.</p>
</div>

